WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.324
The back office team has an invoice extractor that works well.

00:00:05.624 --> 00:00:09.954
One day, someone notices the prompt says: extract the total amount.

00:00:10.254 --> 00:00:15.455
And they change it to: extract the total amount, with two decimal places.

00:00:16.105 --> 00:00:17.777
It looks harmless.

00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:20.072
The deploy goes out on a Friday.

00:00:20.722 --> 00:00:22.726
On Monday, finance complains.

00:00:23.026 --> 00:00:27.066
Invoices with a discount are coming in at the gross amount.

00:00:27.366 --> 00:00:31.434
A one sentence change altered how the model read the field.

00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:36.483
And nobody could have known beforehand, because the only test was opening three

00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:38.046
files and looking at them.

00:00:38.696 --> 00:00:41.301
With regular code, you'd have a unit test.

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:47.335
With a model, the same input can produce different answers, and correct isn't always

00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:48.359
an exact match.

00:00:48.659 --> 00:00:51.530
The test built for that world is called an eval.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:56.887
In this first lesson of the evals and reliability trail, you'll learn what one is

00:00:56.887 --> 00:01:01.818
made of, how to grade it, and how to read its numbers without fooling yourself.

00:01:03.018 --> 00:01:05.528
An eval is a test for model output.

00:01:05.828 --> 00:01:07.223
It has three parts.

00:01:07.873 --> 00:01:08.927
A set of cases.

00:01:09.227 --> 00:01:12.067
Inputs, each with the expected result.

00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:15.161
A way to run the system on all of them.

00:01:15.811 --> 00:01:19.778
And a grading rule, that turns each output into a score.

00:01:20.978 --> 00:01:26.174
The cases come from real data, anonymized, with the expected answer labeled.

00:01:26.824 --> 00:01:31.281
The whole system runs, with the same prompt, model and tools as production.

00:01:31.931 --> 00:01:36.019
Each case produces an output, stored together with the prompt version.

00:01:36.669 --> 00:01:40.000
Grading compares the output with the expected result.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.274
Each case gets a score.

00:01:42.574 --> 00:01:45.990
Pass or fail, or a value from zero to one.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.326
And the report shows the overall rate, the rate per category, and the difference

00:01:51.326 --> 00:01:52.849
from the previous version.

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:57.396
Friday's three files failed at step one, and at step six.

00:01:57.696 --> 00:02:00.263
Three examples by eye aren't a set.

00:02:00.563 --> 00:02:02.628
And looking isn't a grading rule.

00:02:02.928 --> 00:02:08.271
On Monday, the team discovered a kind of invoice, one with a discount, that wasn't

00:02:08.271 --> 00:02:09.314
among the three.

00:02:10.514 --> 00:02:12.347
There are three ways to grade.

00:02:12.647 --> 00:02:14.038
Exact match.

00:02:14.338 --> 00:02:19.167
Code compares field by field, or checks that the output fits a schema.

00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:24.813
Use it when there's a right answer, like a classification, an extraction, or a

00:02:24.813 --> 00:02:25.841
database query.

00:02:26.141 --> 00:02:31.249
On the invoice extractor, the total is an exact match against the labeled number.

00:02:31.899 --> 00:02:33.196
A rule in code.

00:02:33.496 --> 00:02:36.805
Small functions in your code check properties of the output.

00:02:37.105 --> 00:02:43.068
Has a citation, stays under a hundred words, never names a competitor.

00:02:43.368 --> 00:02:47.676
Use it when the text varies, but there are objective criteria.

00:02:48.326 --> 00:02:50.250
And a model as judge.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.109
Another model scores the output, with a written rubric.

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:56.635
Use it for open-ended quality.

00:02:56.935 --> 00:02:58.785
Tone, helpfulness.

00:02:59.435 --> 00:03:02.747
The rule is to start with the simplest one that does the job.

00:03:03.047 --> 00:03:06.054
Exact match is cheap, fast, and never wrong.

00:03:06.354 --> 00:03:09.598
A judge costs money, and it needs evaluating too.

00:03:09.898 --> 00:03:14.124
It inherits the model's variation, and it can agree with the mistake.

00:03:14.774 --> 00:03:16.554
Here's a case where that matters.

00:03:16.854 --> 00:03:20.904
A tone eval for emails used only a model as judge.

00:03:21.204 --> 00:03:26.568
And the judge approved emails that promised a discount the store doesn't offer.

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:28.418
A bigger judge wouldn't fix it.

00:03:28.718 --> 00:03:31.283
It can still find the promise helpful.

00:03:31.583 --> 00:03:33.291
Add rules in code first.

00:03:33.591 --> 00:03:37.479
Don't invent a price, don't name a competitor.

00:03:37.779 --> 00:03:40.478
Then use the judge on what remains.

00:03:41.678 --> 00:03:43.101
Here's one for you.

00:03:43.401 --> 00:03:46.480
The extractor gets forty-eight of fifty cases right.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:51.452
Both misses are discounted invoices, the only two of that kind in the set.

00:03:51.752 --> 00:03:54.436
The team celebrates ninety-six percent.

00:03:54.736 --> 00:03:56.288
What is that number hiding?

00:03:59.338 --> 00:04:01.556
It's hiding the rate per category.

00:04:01.856 --> 00:04:06.255
On the kind of invoice finance fears most, the rate is zero for two.

00:04:06.555 --> 00:04:12.016
The overall number diluted exactly the case that breaks the accounting system.

00:04:12.666 --> 00:04:14.898
And calling it noise doesn't help.

00:04:15.198 --> 00:04:20.568
It might be noise overall, and it's still a hundred percent failure on discounts.

00:04:20.868 --> 00:04:24.057
You need more of those cases, not a pat on the back.

00:04:24.357 --> 00:04:28.910
Fill that slice, fix it, and only then look at the overall number.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.882
There are three sizes of eval.

00:04:32.182 --> 00:04:33.253
A unit case.

00:04:33.553 --> 00:04:36.513
A bug seen in production becomes a case.

00:04:36.813 --> 00:04:39.474
It's the equivalent of a regression test.

00:04:40.124 --> 00:04:41.710
An eval dataset.

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:46.502
From fifty to a few hundred cases covering your product's categories.

00:04:46.802 --> 00:04:48.552
It runs on every change.

00:04:49.202 --> 00:04:51.060
And a public benchmark.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.727
Well known sets that measure models in general.

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:57.243
They help you pick a candidate model.

00:04:57.543 --> 00:04:59.869
They don't tell you whether your system works.

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:05.961
If product wants to swap models because a public benchmark rose two points, and your

00:05:05.961 --> 00:05:09.271
internal eval dropped one point, your eval wins.

00:05:09.571 --> 00:05:10.951
You don't ship a benchmark.

00:05:11.251 --> 00:05:12.807
You ship your classifier.

00:05:13.457 --> 00:05:15.171
And here's the beginner trap.

00:05:15.471 --> 00:05:17.707
Treating the dataset as a dead list.

00:05:18.007 --> 00:05:20.393
The best cases are born in production.

00:05:20.693 --> 00:05:22.683
The invoice finance sent back.

00:05:22.983 --> 00:05:25.880
The ticket the classifier routed to the wrong team.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:29.071
Each incident becomes one more line in the file.

00:05:29.371 --> 00:05:32.786
Six months later, that file is worth more than the prompt.

00:05:33.086 --> 00:05:35.655
You can rewrite a prompt in an afternoon.

00:05:35.955 --> 00:05:38.421
A good dataset takes months to gather.

00:05:39.621 --> 00:05:43.128
Because output varies, a single run can mislead.

00:05:43.428 --> 00:05:48.929
For important cases, run three to five times, and look at the pass rate, not one

00:05:48.929 --> 00:05:49.961
round's result.

00:05:50.611 --> 00:05:55.953
A drop from ninety-two to ninety percent on fifty cases is a single case.

00:05:56.253 --> 00:05:57.399
It may be noise.

00:05:58.049 --> 00:06:01.834
And fifty typical cases won't cover a rare category.

00:06:02.134 --> 00:06:07.636
If discounted invoices are five percent of volume, and you sampled twenty files from

00:06:07.636 --> 00:06:10.910
the top of the folder, that kind may never get in.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:12.090
Stratify.

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:17.869
Include some of every category the business fears, instead of only what shows up

00:06:17.869 --> 00:06:18.280
first.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:22.810
On the page there's an eval that runs in a few seconds.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:27.535
Five customer messages, each labeled billing, order or other.

00:06:28.185 --> 00:06:33.575
The model classifies each one, and code counts how many match the label exactly.

00:06:34.225 --> 00:06:39.669
Now change the prompt to: answer with the category and a short justification.

00:06:39.969 --> 00:06:44.594
Run it again, and all five fail, even though the category at the front of the

00:06:44.594 --> 00:06:45.676
sentence is right.

00:06:46.326 --> 00:06:48.097
That's not the model getting worse.

00:06:48.397 --> 00:06:50.979
Your grading is coupled to the old format.

00:06:51.279 --> 00:06:54.388
The eval and the prompt are the same contract.

00:06:54.688 --> 00:07:00.281
If the output gains a reason, grading reads the category field, not the sentence.

00:07:00.581 --> 00:07:01.590
That's the point.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:07.701
An eval turns I think it got better into a number, and shows you right away what a

00:07:07.701 --> 00:07:08.623
change broke.

00:07:09.823 --> 00:07:11.066
So what changes?

00:07:11.366 --> 00:07:15.920
No prompt, model or tool change ships without running the eval first.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:20.835
Trying five questions by hand before a deploy isn't an eval.

00:07:21.135 --> 00:07:23.746
That's a smell called vibe check eval.

00:07:24.396 --> 00:07:29.724
And the eval has to run on its own, in your pipeline, or with a single command.

00:07:30.024 --> 00:07:32.845
That pattern is called an eval harness.

00:07:33.495 --> 00:07:35.778
One last case.

00:07:36.078 --> 00:07:39.632
Your pipeline runs eighty cases on every pull request.

00:07:39.932 --> 00:07:44.228
Someone also tried four more questions in a chat on Friday, and shipped.

00:07:44.528 --> 00:07:45.899
The pipeline passed.

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:48.582
And finance still came back with a complaint.

00:07:48.882 --> 00:07:49.954
Where did it leak?

00:07:50.254 --> 00:07:51.683
In those four questions.

00:07:51.983 --> 00:07:56.520
If they were different from the eighty, they should have become cases before the

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:56.860
merge.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:01.070
If they weren't, the chat added nothing but false confidence.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.255
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:08:04.555 --> 00:08:06.744
An eval is how you stop guessing.

00:08:07.044 --> 00:08:12.420
Real cases with the right answer, the real system running on them, and a grade that

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:14.493
doesn't depend on anyone's mood.

00:08:14.793 --> 00:08:20.448
Read it per category, run it on every change, and feed it with every incident.

00:08:21.098 --> 00:08:26.266
In the next lesson, we'll look at what happens when a team doesn't have one: the

00:08:26.266 --> 00:08:27.299
untested prompt.

00:08:27.599 --> 00:08:28.429
See you there.
